  "query_preview": "..A Mesoscale Convective System associated with a\nSSW to NNE oriented upper-level jet is currently moving through \nsouthwest Utah as of this writing. This is bringing a number of \nmorning Flash Flood Warnings to various sensitive areas including\nZion National Park. This MCS is moving into an environment in \ncentral Utah with 500-1000 J/Kg of MUCAPE per the SPC \nMesoanalysis, along with 25kts of bulk shear (closer to 50kts near\nthe jet nose itself). Combined with an existing pool of PWATs \nnear an inch and surface dewpoints in the mid to upper 50s, this \nMCS is efficiently wringing out rainfall. A Flood Watch for flash \nflooding continues through midnight for southwest Utah and the \nsouthern/central mountains (the latter largely due to recent burn \nscars).\n\nMoving forward through today, looking at CAMs the RAP/HRRR have \nthe best handle on the MCS location and intensity; they advance it\nnorthward along the I-15 corridor and mountains to the east of \nI-15 through at least Millard County, thus suggesting a threat to \nthe Cottonwood and Widemouth 2 burn scars in addition to other \nrecent burn scars. Beyond central Utah, CAM confidence breaks down\nin terms of how well it holds together tracking farther north. \nFor what it's worth, the RAP has the best handle on the current \nlocation and is depicting the MCS holding together all the \nway through the Wasatch Front / Wasatch Back and up to the UT/ID\nborder, reaching the southern/central Wasatch front around 2-4pm\nand up to the northern Wasatch Front and Cache Valley by 6pm. Will\ncontinue to monitor this MCS through the afternoon. \n\nIn the wake of today's jet/shortwave, much drier air moves into\nthe region overnight into tomorrow along with synoptic subsidence. \nPWATs dropping closer to a half inch. CAMs depict isolated \nconvective initiation in the typical terrain-focused areas, though\none thing to watch for based on past experience is monsoonal \nmoisture tends to hold on a little longer than models expect, so \nwould not be surprised if convective initiation is more \nwidespread than currently shown. In that case, would expect \ninitiation in any areas favored by southwest flow, depending on \nthe degree of overall subsidence.\n\nExpect a similar trend of isolated to scattered convection Sunday\nalong with temperatures warming around 5 degrees as heights build\nover the Central Rockies. A generally dry westerly flow aloft \nthen sets in for Monday through next week, bringing drier and \nwarmer conditions. Any drying/warming can bring a return of \nelevated fire weather concerns, depending on whether any weak \nshortwave troughs can bring elevated winds as well. As of now this\nappears unlikely, so the chance of critical fire weather \nconditions is isolated over pockets of mainly central and southern\nUtah. \n\nThe next round of monsoonal moisture is highly uncertain from \nlooking at various ensembles. The general theme is many members\nslowly bring monsoonal moisture back into the area lake next week\nand on through August, though there is little agreement on timing.",
